red and resigned in the end.", "moments": [{"turn": 14, "text": "The 14k's B4 lost 14.3 points and swung the lead from +0.8 to +15.1 for Black. The engine preferred D8 here. This one gift from the 14k set up everything that followed."}, {"turn": 15, "text": "Opus 5.5 answered with B3, which lost 22.0 points and handed the lead back, from +15.1 to -6.9. The engine's choice was E7. A winning position vanished in a single move."}, {"turn": 16, "text": "The 14k returned the favour with C8, losing 15.7 points and putting Black back at +8.8. Both sides took turns dropping the game here, and Black came out ahead again."}, {"turn": 29, "text": "Opus 5.5 held +13.6 before F2, which lost 16.9 points and dropped the lead to -3.3. The engine wanted G6. After White's E1 the lead was back at +12.3, but the same kind of mistake came again."}, {"turn": 31, "text": "D3 was the worst move of the game for Opus 5.5, losing 23.7 points and turning +12.3 into -11.4. The engine's choice was H5. The 14k's F1 next lost 17.4 points itself, but Black stayed behind at +5.9 only briefly before slipping to -6.0 after F4."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py only flood-fills groups on a pasted board and prints their stones, liberties and a DANGER flag for groups with two or fewer liberties, and count.py only counts stones and enclosed territory after removing named dead stones. Both work on the current position, and neither ranks moves, scores candidates, or plays any continuation.", "stop": false}}